00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.290
Testing is the process by which the
code of a program is checked to see

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.330
that it works by executing the program
with the aim of finding any errors.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.100
If testing is done successfully then
it should be possible to identify and

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.220
remove all the errors from the software.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.180
There are many types of testing which
occur at various stages of development,

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.370
such as during the initial coding,
beta-testing before deployment, and

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.779
after software roll-out during updates.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:35.229
Software testing can also happen with
different focus, from individual units

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.710
of code [unit testing] by software
developers, to testing how software fares

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.930
under different stresses [volume testing].

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.350
Broadly speaking, software testing
falls into three categories:

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:50.220
Functional testing, which ensures
that the software operates in

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.680
line with the specifications.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:55.419
Non-functional testing, to test
aspects which are not related

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:59.010
to software architecture such
as performance and reliability

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:04.720
And Maintenance testing, involving testing
post-release to ensure compatibility

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.720
with new hardware or software

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.650
So why is it important to test code?

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:13.840
Testing is a fundamental
process that helps to create

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.610
reliable and usable software.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:21.100
It helps to check adaptability,
identify errors, avoid future extra

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.179
costs and risks, and ultimately
gives confidence in the program.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.359
So, let’s look at some of the
important features related to testing.

00:01:30.279 --> 00:01:31.690
Firstly it ‘saves time’.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.240
Timely identification of errors
or bugs means they do not affect

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.920
any release dates or deadlines.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:43.719
Also, it ‘improves efficiency’ as errors
which are caught early on are easier

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:47.969
to remedy, meaning less production
time is wasted as there is not as much

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:49.860
software to trace the error back through.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.500
It also helps improve
‘cost effectiveness’.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.489
Efficient testing means less time
spent solving issues and money lost on

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:03.560
revisions, and this can help prevent
costly failures, as system failures can

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:08.069
have extreme safety and subsequently
financial costs depending on the software.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.640
It ‘improves security’ as
new security threats emerge.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.630
Products must be security tested to make
sure that they are protected against

00:02:16.639 --> 00:02:21.210
the latest threats, and by testing when
updating, they can keep up with any

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.840
compatibility requirements involved
with updated operating systems etc.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.570
And finally, it helps with ‘consumer
confidence’ as customers are unlikely

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.949
to be satisfied if they are delivered
buggy, dysfunctional software and by

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.340
testing and eliminating bugs the overall
quality of the product in improved.

00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:43.200
Long term it can also help
retain a products viability,

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.920
otherwise it may become obsolete.

